      "isCustom": false,
      "isFavorited": true,
      "allowsBodyweight": true,
      "muscles": [
        "Chest"
      ],
      "name": "Push Up",
      "notes": "The push-up is a fundamental bodyweight exercise that targets the chest, arms, and shoulders while engaging the core for stability. It requires no equipment and is excellent for building upper body strength.\n\nInstructions:\n\n1. Start in a plank position with hands placed slightly wider than shoulder-width, feet together, and body in a straight line from head to heels.\n2. Engage your core and lower your body by bending your elbows, keeping them close to your body or flared slightly outward.\n3. Lower until your chest nearly touches the ground while maintaining a neutral spine.\n4. Push through your palms to return to the starting position, fully extending your arms."
    },
    {
      "categories": [
        "calisthenics",
        "strength"
      ],
      "equipment": [
        "Weight vest"
      ],
      "icon": "figure.core.training",
      "isCustom": true,
      "isFavorited": true,
      "allowsBodyweight": true,
      "muscles": [
        "Chest"
      ],
      "name": "Archer Push-Up",
      "notes": "The push-up is a fundamental bodyweight exercise that targets the chest, arms, and shoulders while engaging the core for stability. It requires no equipment and is excellent for building upper body strength.\n\nInstructions:\n\n1. Start in a plank position with hands placed slightly wider than shoulder-width, feet together, and body in a straight line from head to heels.\n2. Engage your core and lower your body by bending your elbows, keeping them close to your body or flared slightly outward.\n3. Lower until your chest nearly touches the ground while maintaining a neutral spine.\n4. Push through your palms to return to the starting position, fully extending your arms."
    },
    {
      "categories": [
        "strength",
        "calisthenics"
      ],
      "equipment": [
        "Bench"
      ],
      "icon": "figure.strengthtraining.traditional",
      "isCustom": false,
      "isFavorited": true,
      "muscles": [
        "Chest"
      ],
      "name": "Push-Ups, Decline",
      "notes": "Decline push-ups are another modified version of the traditional push-up that target the upper body muscles in a different way. To perform a decline push-up, elevate your feet on an elevated surface, such as a bench, chair, or step, while placing your hands on the ground in a push-up position. Lower your body towards the ground while maintaining a straight line from your shoulders to your ankles, and then push back up to the starting position.\nUnlike the inclined push-up, the decline push-up places more emphasis on the shoulders and triceps, while still engaging the chest muscles to a lesser extent. By elevating your feet, you increase the difficulty of the exercise by placing more weight on your upper body, forcing your shoulders and triceps to work harder to push your body back up. The decline push-up can be a great way to challenge your upper body strength and improve your ability to perform other push-up variations. As with any exercise, be sure to use proper form and start with a height that is appropriate for your strength and fitness level."
    },
    {
      "categories": [
        "strength"
